\begin{proof}
Let $U=\mathbb{Z}^2$. It is nonempty since $(0,0)\in U$. If $(a,b),(c,d)\in U$, then $(a+c,b+d)\in\mathbb{Z}^2$, so $U$ is closed under addition. If $(a,b)\in U$, then $(-a,-b)\in\mathbb{Z}^2$, so $U$ is closed under additive inverses. However, $(1,0)\in U$ while $\frac12(1,0)=(\frac12,0)\notin U$. Thus $U$ is not closed under scalar multiplication and therefore is not a subspace of $\mathbf{R}^2$.
\end{proof}
